BT /F1 10 Tf 50 720 Td (IN THE MADRAS HIGH COURT \(PRINCIPAL SEAT, CHENNAI\)
AT CHENNAI

CASE NO: Crl.O.P. No. 8912 of 2026
CNR NUMBER: MHC010456122026
DECISION DATE: 2026-01-29
YEAR: 2026

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Justice G. Jayachandran

PARTIES:
Dr. R. Ramanathan, Former Dean, Government Medical College
... Appellant\(s\) / Petitioner\(s\)
Versus
Inspector of Police, Vigilance and Anti-Corruption, Chennai
... Respondent\(s\) / State

STATUTORY PROVISIONS & ACTS REFERRED:
Prevention of Corruption Act 1988, Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita 2023, Code of Criminal Procedure 1973

SECTIONS & CLAUSES UNDER CONSIDERATION:
PC Act Section 13\(1\)\(b\), PC Act Section 17A, BNSS Section 528, CrPC Section 482

KEY LEGAL DOMAINS & TOPICS:
Quashing of FIR, Vigilance and Anti-Corruption, Prior Approval, Section 17A PC Act, Abuse of Process

================================================================================
                                   JUDGMENT
================================================================================

1. FACTUAL MATRIX:
DVAC registered FIR against retired medical college Dean without seeking prior approval from the Competent Authority under Section 17A of Prevention of Corruption \(Amendment\) Act 2018.

2. ISSUES FRAMED FOR ADJUDICATION:
1. Whether prior approval under Section 17A of PC Act is mandatory before registering FIR regarding official administrative decisions?

3. STATUTORY FRAMEWORK & PRECEDENTS:
Section 17A safeguards public servants from vexatious prosecution by mandating prior approval before conducting inquiry or investigation.

4. RATIO DECIDENDI:
Investigation commenced without mandatory prior approval under Section 17A of Prevention of Corruption Act is illegal and liable to be quashed under Section 482 CrPC / Section 528 BNSS.

5. FINAL DISPOSITION & ORDER:
Criminal Original Petition Allowed; FIR in Crime No. 04/2025 quashed for lack of valid sanction under Section 17A of PC Act.

Decreed and ordered accordingly.

Hon'ble Justice G. Jayach) Tj ET
